    {"id": 12, "type": "manual",
     "desc": "Open explanation, part (b). Priya wants to redraw the leaf plot with only the lengths that actually occurred, spaced out evenly and equally far apart, so that 1/4 sits next to 1/2 next to 3/4 next to 1 next to 1 1/4 with the same gap between each pair. Here that particular relabelling happens to keep the spacing honest because the five lengths really are a quarter of an inch apart. The child must explain what the scale on a line plot has to promise: equal distances along the line must stand for equal differences in length, so a mark may only be dropped if nothing is being squeezed. Accept any answer that says the spacing has to match the sizes (so a missing length would have to leave a gap, not close one up), or that gives a concrete example of what goes wrong if an unused length is closed up. Do not accept an answer that only says Priya is right or wrong without saying what the spacing means.",
